WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.520
Near-death experiences, hypothermia,&nbsp;
and an icebreaker no one saw coming. Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:08.154
and then there was that time&nbsp;
the whole crew was drugged.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:13.080
One of the most bizarre things to happen on the&nbsp;
"Titanic" set happened while filming the framing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:17.760
narrative centered around Bill Paxton's character&nbsp;
Brock Lovett and his search of the wreck of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:22.740
Titanic. Marilyn McAvoy — a background painter&nbsp;
who worked on "Titanic" — spoke to Vice about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:27.780
the incident, which occurred in 1996 when some&nbsp;
cast and crew sat down to enjoy a clam chowder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:33.360
lunch. There was only one problem: It was&nbsp;
spiked with PCP. Those affected — including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:38.520
James Cameron and actor Bill Paxton — were rushed&nbsp;
to the hospital. Both Kate Winslet and Leonardo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:43.122
DiCaprio were spared this eventful dinner, since&nbsp;
they weren't required for this Canadian shoot.

00:00:43.122 --> 00:00:45.420
"And he said, 'Well did you eat the clam chowder?'

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:50.534
And I thought, 'Well yeah, I had a couple of&nbsp;
bowls,' and about that time I started feeling it."

00:00:50.534 --> 00:00:55.440
Marilyn McAvoy recalls how the evening began,&nbsp;
with people going for seconds and thirds of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:00.300
chowder because it tasted so good. Everyone was&nbsp;
having a lot more than they usually would. Soon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:04.860
people started tripping, and once they realized&nbsp;
what was happening, Cameron allegedly went up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:10.080
his room to make himself throw up and stop the&nbsp;
hallucinogen from taking over. McAvoy described&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:14.640
how the crew was in the hospital all night, as&nbsp;
some people were having a bad trip. She said,

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:18.720
"It became pretty chaotic. ... People had&nbsp;
a lot of energy. Some were in wheelchairs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:21.360
flying down the hallways. ...&nbsp;
People were playing hacky sack."

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:24.540
Bill Paxton also opened up to&nbsp;
Larry King about the incident,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:28.224
describing the hospital scene&nbsp;
that night in much the same way.

00:01:28.224 --> 00:01:32.126
"You see some people are freaking out, some people&nbsp;
are congo dancing, some people -

00:01:32.126 --> 00:01:32.966
"What happened to you?"

00:01:32.966 --> 00:01:34.225
are euphoric."

00:01:34.483 --> 00:01:36.900
"I knew I was pretty stoned."

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:40.500
On her end, McAvoy said it all seemed&nbsp;
like a dream, and that her experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:44.280
wasn't too bad. Unfortunately for the&nbsp;
crew, they never caught the perpetrator,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:48.910
resulting in that entire night going down&nbsp;
as one of the weirdest on-set moments.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:54.060
Once the Titanic sinks, Jack freezes in the&nbsp;
frigid waters, but Rose manages to stay alive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:58.260
and flag down a nearby boat looking&nbsp;
for survivors. It's a tragic ending,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:02.520
but Rose's shivering tears weren't pure&nbsp;
acting. It was cold as hell in that water.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:06.600
In 2017, Kate Winslet appeared on&nbsp;
"The Late Show With Stephen Colbert"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:10.140
and revealed that when shooting this&nbsp;
scene in the water, she got hypothermia.

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:12.900
"Yeah, I was really f—ing cold!"

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:16.920
She further explained that, even though this&nbsp;
was all supposed to be pretend movie magic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:20.640
there was so much water that the&nbsp;
crew couldn't heat it all up. Hey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:25.080
maybe if DiCaprio had just climbed up on&nbsp;
that piece of wood to keep her warm… nah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:29.216
nevermind. There's no way there&nbsp;
was room for him. No possible way.

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:34.080
It turns out, Kate Winslet suffered quite&nbsp;
a bit during filming. In a 1997 interview&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:38.940
with The Los Angeles Times, Winslet revealed&nbsp;
a variety of injuries she got on set: bruises,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:43.620
gashes, and a chipped elbow. She also described&nbsp;
the moment when she almost drowned during one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:47.400
scene. What makes this even more bizarre&nbsp;
is the fact that Cameron didn't notice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:51.480
and kept directing, as he was completely&nbsp;
oblivious to her near-death experience.

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:55.200
The scene in question takes place when Rose&nbsp;
and Jack are running through the ship as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.200 --> 00:03:00.540
water is gushing in. They try to find an escape&nbsp;
route, but soon get trapped in by a closed gate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:05.160
with water pouring in on them. Once the scene&nbsp;
was cut, the production team opened the gate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:10.500
to allow DiCaprio and Winslet through. However,&nbsp;
her coat got caught on the iron bars and she was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:15.060
submerged in the water. Winslet described the&nbsp;
experience to The Los Angeles Times, saying,

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:18.300
"I had no breath left. I thought&nbsp;
I'd burst. And Jim just said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:22.987
'OK, let's go again.' That was his attitude. I&nbsp;
didn't want to be a wimp so I didn't complain."

00:03:22.987 --> 00:03:27.780
"Well, I'm fine. I'll be fine, really."

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:31.560
Although Cameron realized a few minutes&nbsp;
later that Winslet was feeling shaken by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:34.680
the experience, he wanted to get back&nbsp;
into it and shoot the scene again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:39.318
comparing it to getting back on a horse&nbsp;
after a fall. The show must go on, after all.

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:44.520
For many, the staircase in "Titanic" is an iconic&nbsp;
symbol of Rose and Jack's love. Early in the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:47.940
Jack waits for Rose at the bottom of&nbsp;
the staircase to escort her to dinner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:51.540
with her family. He's blown away when&nbsp;
he sees her looking fancy and beautiful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:55.500
and the romantic tension between them is&nbsp;
palpable. In the final scene of the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.500 --> 00:04:00.720
Rose passes away and the staircase appears&nbsp;
again, reuniting her with Jack and all of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:05.400
others lost on the Titanic. Both are beautiful&nbsp;
and emotional moments, as they show the journey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:09.660
of Jack and Rose's relationship, culminating&nbsp;
with their final embrace in the afterlife.

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:13.740
However, behind the scenes of this&nbsp;
romantic tale is a puzzling story.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:19.440
In 2017, Cameron reflected on his time filming&nbsp;
"Titanic" in National Geographic's "Titanic:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:24.120
20 Years Later with James Cameron." When looking&nbsp;
back at the iconic staircase, Cameron recalled,

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:28.740
"The staircase has got a steel footing, then&nbsp;
when we sank the ship, it lifted. Wood is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:32.520
buoyant. It ripped off that footing and it&nbsp;
all floated up, and it actually pinned two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:36.180
stunt players. Fortunately, they weren't&nbsp;
hurt, but it was a pretty scary moment."

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:39.540
The staircase was built to replicate&nbsp;
the actual grand stairs that were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:43.624
aboard the famous vessel, so perhaps it&nbsp;
brought some of its bad luck with it.

00:04:43.624 --> 00:04:48.180
Brett Baker — Leonardo DiCaprio's photo&nbsp;
double in "Titanic" — spoke to Vanity Fair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:53.280
in 2016 about his experience making the film.&nbsp;
Baker was a struggling actor and was thrilled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:57.780
to join the project, even though the role wasn't&nbsp;
exactly what he'd trained for. As a photo double,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:02.520
Baker's job was to silently take DiCaprio's&nbsp;
place in shots where you couldn't see his face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:07.920
to give the star a break from more tedious work.&nbsp;
Baker's presence in "Titanic" is undetectable, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:12.300
he remembers how nervous he was to meet Cameron&nbsp;
for the first time since he is both shorter and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:17.040
older than DiCaprio. Luckily, Cameron believed he&nbsp;
looked enough like DiCaprio that it would work.

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:21.900
Baker looks back fondly on his time on the&nbsp;
"Titanic" set, saying most actors don't get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:26.400
their big break starring in one of the biggest&nbsp;
films of all time. Plus, the photo double life got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:31.200
interesting thanks to all of the teen fans waiting&nbsp;
by the studio gate, hoping to catch a glimpse of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:36.420
DiCaprio. During the filming of "Titanic," the&nbsp;
Leo-craze was in full swing. Security would usher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:40.860
DiCaprio out the back to avoid the fans, and Baker&nbsp;
would be brought out front for everyone to gush&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:45.960
over, turning him into a real-life photo double&nbsp;
for the star. As Baker recalled to Vanity Fair,

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:50.220
"[The fans] went berserk. Screaming, looking&nbsp;
inside the window, waving their pieces of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:54.300
paper for an autograph. ... I don't know if anyone&nbsp;
knew that was going on at the gate every morning."

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:57.713
At least he got his own little&nbsp;
fan club during filming.

00:05:57.713 --> 00:06:02.040
Actors have been known to take things from&nbsp;
set from time to time. Think of it less as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:05.760
stealing and more as holding on to a piece of&nbsp;
memorabilia that commemorates the time they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:09.240
spent filming. It's understandable&nbsp;
when one goes through blood, sweat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:12.360
and tears to make a film that they&nbsp;
feel like they deserve a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:15.720
something for their troubles ... aside&nbsp;
from their massive paycheck, of course.

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:20.760
Winslet did almost drown and get hypothermia.&nbsp;
Maybe it's fair to say that she wanted to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:24.180
a little something extra on the way out.&nbsp;
In an interview with The New York Times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:26.580
she revealed that she likes&nbsp;
saving things from sets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:29.760
and during "Titanic," she snagged&nbsp;
some jewelry. She explained,

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:32.880
"I did take a pair of Rose's&nbsp;
earrings, but somewhere I lost one."

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:36.780
It's a fitting story, since "Titanic"&nbsp;
itself is framed around a search for lost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:40.678
jewelry – a necklace that, of course,&nbsp;
gets lost in the ocean at the end.

00:06:40.678 --> 00:06:45.540
It's no secret that Leonardo DiCaprio and&nbsp;
Kate Winslet have enjoyed a decades-long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:48.840
friendship. Ever since starring as&nbsp;
star-crossed lovers in "Titanic,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:52.980
the two have freely shown each other their&nbsp;
admiration and support over the years. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:57.900
decade after "Titanic," the two starred in the&nbsp;
post-war romantic drama "Revolutionary Road."

00:06:57.900 --> 00:07:01.440
Winslet talked about her friendship with&nbsp;
DiCaprio in an interview with The Guardian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:04.620
noting that they've known each other&nbsp;
for half their lives. She said,

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:07.860
"He's my friend, my really close&nbsp;
friend. We're bonded for life."

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:11.280
Winslet won the Golden Globe&nbsp;
for Best Actress in 2009 for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:15.180
"Revolutionary Road" and gushed about&nbsp;
her co-star in her off-the-cuff speech.

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:18.780
For his part, DiCaprio has described&nbsp;
how Winslet broke the ice between the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.780 --> 00:07:21.840
two right off the bat. In the official&nbsp;
behind-the-scenes book of the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:26.940
Ed W. Marsh's "James Cameron's Titanic," DiCaprio&nbsp;
shared their bizarre first encounter that did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:32.220
just that. The first scene he shot was the one&nbsp;
where he draws naked Rose. DiCaprio explained,

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:36.780
"[Winslet] had no shame with it ... She wanted to&nbsp;
break the ice a little beforehand, so she flashed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:41.340
me. I wasn't prepared for that, so she had one&nbsp;
up on me. I was pretty comfortable after that."

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:46.080
A 1998 Rolling Stone profile on Winslet&nbsp;
detailed how things snowballed from there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:49.680
with Winslet and DiCaprio spending the&nbsp;
rest of the shoot pranking each other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:53.460
and forming a very comfortable&nbsp;
relationship. Winslet explained,

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:56.520
"We were kind of the two goofy kids&nbsp;
on the set [...] We’d do the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.520 --> 00:07:59.400
ridiculous things to each other.&nbsp;
He’d be tickling me, groping me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:03.060
winding me up. And I’d be doing the same&nbsp;
thing back, sort of grabbing his bum."

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:10.740
DiCaprio discussed the seriousness of&nbsp;
their friendship on set as well, saying,

00:08:10.740 --> 00:08:14.880
"She was my best friend for seven months. We’d&nbsp;
unload the stresses of the shoot to each other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:17.400
vent to each other, watch out for each other. Kate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:20.700
was just the perfect person to work with&nbsp;
because she was very much one of the guys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:24.000
and it would have been much harder&nbsp;
without her. We were partners."

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:27.360
"Oh that was better. You gotta work on it. Really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:30.776
try and hawk it up and get some&nbsp;
body to it. You know, you gotta…"

